========================================================================================
 Audit Volume Attribution, in SPL
========================================================================================
 The question these answer: an estate is pushing 100M+ audit events a day into a SIEM,
 the ingest bill is a line item somebody has to defend, and nobody can say WHAT is
 producing the volume. Without that, "filter the audit log" is a guess, and a guess
 about audit data is the kind of thing that gets declined in a compliance review.

 These searches attribute volume to mount, path shape, operation, namespace, and
 identity, and then rank the high-volume / low-forensic-value patterns so a filtering
 decision can be argued from evidence instead of vibes.

 STATUS: verified 2026-09-02 against Splunk 10.4.3. 10 of 13 searches return rows,
 and 11 of the 12 companion dashboard panels do. The exceptions are two scheduled
 rollups run outside their window, and the classifier-gap panel, which is correctly
 empty when the classifier covers every path present.

 Assumes the sample loaded as index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit with KV_MODE=json.
 Against your own estate the index and sourcetype will differ; only the first line of
 each search changes.

 Nested JSON fields are referenced as 'request.path' (single quotes) inside eval/where,
 and as request.path=... in the base search.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 TWO RULES THAT DECIDE WHETHER THE NUMBERS ARE RIGHT
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

 1. COUNT BOTH type="request" AND type="response".
    Vault emits one audit entry when a request arrives and a second when it is answered.
    Both are written to the device, both are shipped, both are billed. Every search in
    this file therefore places NO type= filter and counts both, because the question is
    ingest cost, not "what happened".
    This is the opposite of stale-secrets.spl, which folds type=response only: there
    the question is "did this secret get read", and counting the request too would
    double every read. Different question, different rule. Do not copy one into the other.

    Where a search does need to separate them, it splits BY 'type' rather than filtering.

    ⚠️ CHECK THIS FIRST: run the request/response split panel before anything else. A
    real audit device emits both, roughly 1:1. If you see only one type, your shipper is
    dropping half the stream, and every volume number here is half the truth while
    looking entirely plausible.

 2. THE ROOT NAMESPACE IS AN EMPTY STRING.
    Any fully-qualified path MUST be built as:
        | eval secret = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"") . 'request.path'
    A bare concatenation of a null/absent namespace yields null and SILENTLY DROPS every
    root-namespace event, with no error and no empty panel to warn you. Measured on one
    real cluster that was 17% of events, and on a test corpus 41%. This bug has shipped
    for real.
    The same applies to grouping BY namespace: relabel "" to a visible token first, or
    the largest namespace in most estates renders as a blank row.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 A NOTE ON SCALE, AND ON tstats
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Never write a volume search that groups BY the secret path. A large estate has hundreds
 of thousands of KV v2 paths; `stats count BY secret` builds a result set the same size
 and answers a question nobody asked. Volume attribution is a question about CLASSES of
 traffic, so every search here groups by mount, mount_type, operation, namespace,
 identity, or a derived path PATTERN, all of which are low cardinality by construction.

 Where tstats genuinely helps: `| tstats count` reads the tsidx metadata directly and
 never touches the raw events, so the total-volume baseline and the per-day trend of
 TOTAL ingest are effectively free at any retention depth. See V0 and V1a.

 Where tstats does NOT help, and why: `request.mount_type` and friends are search-time
 JSON extractions, not indexed fields. tstats can only group by indexed fields
 (index, sourcetype, source, host, _time) or by fields in an accelerated data model.
 So every attribution search below is a normal search. If you run this daily on a large
 estate, the right move is the same one as 1.4: a scheduled search that folds into a
 summary index, and a dashboard that reads the summary. See V8.
========================================================================================


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 V0  Sanity check: total ingest, and the request/response split
     RUN THIS FIRST. A real audit device emits request and response entries roughly
     1:1. If request_events is 0, your audit device or forwarder is dropping request
     entries, every volume number here is half of the real one, and your SIEM bill
     will disagree with this dashboard.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| tstats count AS total_events WHERE index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit

 ...and the split, which needs the raw events because 'type' is not indexed:
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
| stats count BY type
| eventstats sum(count) AS total
| eval pct = round(count * 100 / total, 1)


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 V1  PANEL 1: Events per day, split by mount type
     The shape of the trend matters more than the height. A flat line is a steady-state
     platform; a step change is a workload that onboarded; a daily spike is a batch job,
     and a batch job is the most commonly filterable thing in the whole estate.
     Both request and response counted (see rule 1).
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
| timechart span=1d count BY request.mount_type useother=t limit=10

 V1a  Total ingest per day only, at any retention depth, for free.
      Use this one when the window is long enough that V1 gets slow. It cannot break
      down by mount type (see the tstats note above), but it will render 18 months
      instantly, and it is the correct denominator to reconcile against the SIEM's own
      licence-usage numbers.
| tstats count WHERE index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit BY _time span=1d


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 V2  PANEL 2: Top mounts by volume, with percent of total
     mount_point is the unit an operator can actually act on: audit device filtering,
     mount tuning, and the conversation with the team that owns it all happen per mount.
     Cardinality is bounded by the number of mounts, not by paths.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
| eval mount = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"") . coalesce('request.mount_point',"(none)")
| stats count AS events,
        dc(auth.display_name) AS distinct_identities,
        values(request.mount_type) AS mount_type
        BY mount
| eventstats sum(events) AS total_events
| eval pct_of_total = round(events * 100 / total_events, 2)
| eval events_per_day = round(events / 30, 0)
| sort - events
| head 25
| table mount mount_type events pct_of_total events_per_day distinct_identities

/*  events_per_day above assumes a 30-day window. If the dashboard time picker says
    something else, that column lies. It is kept because "per day" is the unit ingest
    is priced in and an operator reads it faster than a raw total; change the divisor
    if you change the default window.                                                  */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 V3  PANEL 3: Top operations by volume
     Split by 'type' as well, because a request/response ratio that is not ~1.0 for a
     given operation is worth a second look: it usually means errors, or an operation
     that fans out.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
| eval op = coalesce('request.operation',"(none)"),
       mt = coalesce('request.mount_type',"(none)")
| stats count AS events BY op mt type
| eventstats sum(events) AS total_events
| eval pct_of_total = round(events * 100 / total_events, 2)
| sort - events
| head 30


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 V4  PANEL 4: Volume by namespace
     Note the relabel. Root is an empty string and would otherwise render as a blank
     row, which reads as a broken panel rather than as the largest tenant.
     This is the panel that answers "whose budget is this".
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
| eval ns = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"")
| eval ns = if(ns == "", "root (empty string)", ns)
| stats count AS events,
        dc(request.mount_point) AS mounts,
        dc(auth.display_name) AS identities
        BY ns
| eventstats sum(events) AS total_events
| eval pct_of_total = round(events * 100 / total_events, 2)
| sort - events


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 V5  PANEL 5: Top talkers, the identities generating the most events
     Volume attribution stops being abstract at this panel: every row is a workload with
     an owner you can go and talk to. A single service account at 20% of total ingest is
     a conversation, not a filter rule.

     display_name is the human-legible one; service_account_name is the Kubernetes
     workload. Both are low cardinality relative to paths. entity_id would be more
     precise and much less readable, so it is not the grouping key here.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
| eval who    = coalesce('auth.display_name',"(unauthenticated / no auth block)"),
       k8s_sa = coalesce('auth.metadata.service_account_name',"-")
| stats count AS events,
        dc(request.mount_point) AS mounts,
        values(request.mount_type) AS mount_types,
        earliest(_time) AS first_seen,
        latest(_time) AS last_seen
        BY who k8s_sa
| eventstats sum(events) AS total_events
| eval pct_of_total = round(events * 100 / total_events, 2)
| eval first_seen = strftime(first_seen,"%F %T"), last_seen = strftime(last_seen,"%F %T")
| sort - events
| head 25
| table who k8s_sa events pct_of_total mounts mount_types first_seen last_seen

/*  A "(unauthenticated / no auth block)" row is not an anomaly. Unauthenticated
    endpoints (sys/health, sys/seal-status, login attempts that failed before an
    identity existed) legitimately have no auth block. If that row is LARGE, it is
    usually a load balancer health check, which is V6's best filter candidate.       */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 V6  PANEL 6, THE MONEY PANEL: filter candidates
     High volume plus low forensic value. This is the panel that turns "our audit logs
     are too big" into "these three path patterns are 60% of your volume, and here is
     what you give up by dropping each one".

     Classification is by PATH PATTERN, not by path. That is deliberate and it is the
     only reason this is safe at 345k-path scale: the case statement collapses an
     unbounded path space into ~a dozen buckets before any stats command runs.

     Forensic value is a judgement, not a measurement. The ratings below are defensible
     defaults, not a recommendation to filter. Read the caveats before acting.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
| eval p  = coalesce('request.path',""),
       mt = coalesce('request.mount_type',""),
       op = coalesce('request.operation',"")

| eval pattern = case(
      mt=="transit" AND match(p,"/(encrypt|decrypt)/"),      "transit encrypt/decrypt (data-plane crypto)",
      mt=="transit" AND match(p,"/(sign|verify|hmac|rewrap)/"), "transit sign/verify/hmac",
      mt=="transit",                                          "transit other",
      match(p,"^auth/token/lookup-self$"),                    "token lookup-self",
      match(p,"^auth/token/renew-self$"),                     "token renew-self",
      match(p,"^auth/token/(lookup|renew|create)"),           "token lookup/renew/create",
      match(p,"^sys/(health|seal-status|leader|ha-status)$"), "sys health / status probes",
      match(p,"^sys/metrics"),                                "sys metrics scrape",
      match(p,"^sys/internal/ui/"),                           "sys internal UI (browser chatter)",
      match(p,"^identity/(lookup|oidc)/"),                    "identity lookup",
      match(p,"^cubbyhole/"),                                 "cubbyhole",
      match(p,"/metadata/") OR match(p,"/subkeys/"),          "kv metadata / subkeys (browsing)",
      op=="list",                                             "list operations (browsing)",
      match(p,"/login$"),                                     "auth login",
      match(p,"/data/"),                                      "kv secret data access",
      true(),                                                 "everything else")

 forensic_value is the question "if this line were missing from the SIEM, would an
 investigation notice?". Anything that mutates state or grants access is HIGH and is
 not a filter candidate at any volume.
| eval forensic_value = case(
      pattern=="transit encrypt/decrypt (data-plane crypto)", "LOW: high-rate data-plane crypto, key usage is provable from metrics",
      pattern=="transit sign/verify/hmac",                    "LOW-MED: same shape, but signing is sometimes in audit scope",
      pattern=="token lookup-self",                           "LOW: a token asking about itself grants nothing",
      pattern=="token renew-self",                            "LOW: renewal of an already-issued token",
      pattern=="sys health / status probes",                  "LOW: load balancer and orchestrator probes",
      pattern=="sys metrics scrape",                          "LOW: monitoring scrape",
      pattern=="sys internal UI (browser chatter)",           "LOW: UI page loads, not access",
      pattern=="kv metadata / subkeys (browsing)",            "MED: browsing, not use, but shows intent",
      pattern=="list operations (browsing)",                  "MED: enumeration is a recon signal, keep if you hunt",
      pattern=="identity lookup",                             "MED",
      pattern=="cubbyhole",                                   "MED",
      pattern=="auth login",                                  "HIGH: authentication events, do not filter",
      pattern=="kv secret data access",                       "HIGH: this is the access record, do not filter",
      true(),                                                 "UNKNOWN: classify before filtering")

| stats count AS events,
        dc(auth.display_name) AS identities,
        dc(request.mount_point) AS mounts
        BY pattern forensic_value
| eventstats sum(events) AS total_events
| eval pct_of_total = round(events * 100 / total_events, 2)

 Running total, so the table answers "how much do the top N patterns buy me" directly.
| sort - events
| streamstats sum(pct_of_total) AS cumulative_pct
| eval cumulative_pct = round(cumulative_pct, 2)
| eval candidate = if(match(forensic_value,"^LOW") AND pct_of_total >= 1,
                      "FILTER CANDIDATE", "keep")
| table pattern forensic_value events pct_of_total cumulative_pct candidate identities mounts

/*  HOW TO READ THIS, AND THE CAVEATS THAT GO WITH IT.

    The headline is the sum of pct_of_total across the FILTER CANDIDATE rows. In estates
    that run transit at data-plane rates, encrypt/decrypt alone is routinely the single
    largest contributor, because every application call to encrypt a field emits a
    request and a response entry regardless of how small the payload is.

    Before anything is filtered:

    - Filtering happens at the audit device or in the log pipeline, NOT in Splunk.
      Excluding events at search time saves nothing; you are already paying to index
      them. The saving is upstream.
    - Vault audit device filtering is a supported feature on Enterprise. If you are on
      Community, the filter belongs in the shipper.
    - ⚠️ A blocked audit device blocks Vault. Audit is fail-closed by design. Never
      test a filter change on the only audit device on a production cluster.
    - ⚠️ Get the retention decision reviewed by whoever owns the control. "Low forensic
      value" is an engineering opinion; the control owner may have a regulatory reason
      to keep something this table calls noise. Bring the percentages, not a conclusion.
    - A cheaper middle path than dropping: route the LOW rows to cold/cheap storage
      instead of the indexed SIEM tier. Same saving, nothing lost.
    - "everything else" being large means the classifier is stale for your estate.
      Run /* MEASURED 2026-09-02, and this is why V7 exists.
   Against a synthetic sample the classifier covered 100% of paths and V7 was
   empty. Against REAL Vault audit data from two different clusters it was not:
     kv update/read   60 events   (paths with no /data/ segment, so KV v1 or similar)
     pki              18
     ssh               4
     aws               2
   The classifier knows KV, auth, transit and sys. A real estate also runs pki,
   ssh, aws and database engines, and those fall straight through. An empty V7
   means "this classifier fits THIS data", never "the classifier is complete".
   Re-run V7 whenever the source changes. */

V7 to see what is in it before you trust any percentage here.               */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 V7  What is inside "everything else"
     Companion to V6. If the unclassified bucket is a meaningful share of volume, the
     case statement is missing a pattern your estate actually runs.

     This groups by mount_type + operation, NOT by path, so it stays bounded. To see
     actual paths, add a mount filter first and keep the window short; a bare
     `BY request.path` over a large estate is the one thing this file tells you not
     to do.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
| eval p = coalesce('request.path',"")
| where NOT (match(p,"^(auth|sys|identity|cubbyhole)/") OR match(p,"/(data|metadata|subkeys|encrypt|decrypt|login)/") OR match(p,"/login$"))
| stats count AS events BY request.mount_type request.operation request.mount_class
| sort - events
| head 30


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 V8  PRODUCTION SHAPE: fold volume into a summary index
     Every search above rescans the window. At 100M+ events/day that is exactly the cost
     problem the dashboard exists to solve, so do not solve it by re-reading everything
     every refresh.

     Schedule hourly. The summary rows are bounded by
     (mounts x operations x namespaces x patterns), which is small and does not grow
     with path count or with event volume. Retain the summary for as long as you want
     the trend, independent of raw audit retention.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit earliest=-1h@h latest=@h
| eval ns = coalesce('request.namespace.path',""),
       p  = coalesce('request.path',""),
       mt = coalesce('request.mount_type',"(none)")
| eval ns = if(ns == "", "root", ns)
| eval pattern = case(
      mt=="transit" AND match(p,"/(encrypt|decrypt)/"),      "transit-crypto",
      match(p,"^auth/token/(lookup|renew)-self$"),           "token-self",
      match(p,"^sys/(health|seal-status|leader|ha-status)$"), "sys-probe",
      match(p,"/metadata/") OR match(p,"/subkeys/"),          "kv-browse",
      match(p,"/login$"),                                     "auth-login",
      match(p,"/data/"),                                      "kv-data",
      true(),                                                 "other")
| stats count AS events BY ns mt pattern request.operation type
| collect index=vault_summary sourcetype=vault:audit:volume

 ...and the dashboard search that reads it back. Cheap regardless of history depth.
index=vault_summary sourcetype=vault:audit:volume
| stats sum(events) AS events BY pattern
| eventstats sum(events) AS total_events
| eval pct_of_total = round(events * 100 / total_events, 2)
| sort - events


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 V9  Reconciliation: does this dashboard agree with the ingest bill?
     Run this before quoting a percentage at anyone. Bytes, not events, is what a SIEM
     charges for, and a transit encrypt entry is much smaller than a KV read entry with
     a response body. A pattern that is 40% of EVENTS may be 15% of BYTES, and the
     saving that gets argued about is the byte number.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
| eval bytes = len(_raw)
| eval mt = coalesce('request.mount_type',"(none)")
| stats count AS events, sum(bytes) AS bytes, avg(bytes) AS avg_bytes BY mt type
| eventstats sum(bytes) AS total_bytes, sum(events) AS total_events
| eval pct_of_events = round(events * 100 / total_events, 2),
       pct_of_bytes  = round(bytes  * 100 / total_bytes,  2),
       avg_bytes     = round(avg_bytes, 0),
       gb            = round(bytes / 1024 / 1024 / 1024, 3)
| sort - bytes
| table mt type events pct_of_events gb pct_of_bytes avg_bytes

/*  len(_raw) is the on-disk raw size before Splunk's own overhead, so this is an
    approximation of licence volume, not an invoice. Compare it against the SIEM's
    native usage reporting rather than replacing it. The point of the panel is the
    RATIO between pct_of_events and pct_of_bytes, which is where a filtering argument
    quietly falls apart.                                                             */
